3. Difference between stored procedure and function

| Stored Procedure                                                                  | Function                                                                             |
| --------------------------------------------------------------------------------  | -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  |
| -A stored procedure is used to execute a set of SQL statements to perform a task. | -A function is used to perform a calculation or operation and return a single value. |
| -It may or may not return a value.                                                | -It must always return a value.                                                      |
| -It is called using the CALL statement.                                           | -It is called inside a SELECT statement or expression.                               |
| -It can use IN, OUT, and INOUT parameters.                                        | -It generally uses input parameters only.                                            |
| -It is mainly used for business logic and data operations.                        | -It is mainly used for calculations and reusable logic.                              |



4. Trigger
Definition:
Trigger is stored program in database which execute automatically to respond specifi events such as 
insert, update, delete, etc.

types of trigger:

before insert
after insert
before update
after update
before delete
after delete

syntax:
delimiter//
create trigger triggername
after insert on tablename
for each row
begin
sql statements;
end//
delimiter;

Example:
delimiter//
create trigger trg_after_insert
after insert on medicine
for each row
begin
insert into sales(customername, medicinename, quantity, rate, total)
values ('auto', new.name, 1, new.rate, new.rate);
end//
delimiter ;

5. View
Definition: 
View is virtual table normally it is use for fetch data from two or more tables but view cannot allocate memory 
it only fetch data and show user.

Syntax:
create view viewname as
select column1, column2
from tablename;

Example:
create view medicineview as
select name, rate
from medicine;

use:
select * from medicineview;

#Difference between simple view and complex view

| simple view                                            | complex view                                                |
| -----------------------------------------------------  | ----------------------------------------------------------  |
| -created using single table                            | -created using multiple tables                              |
| -does not use join operation                           | -uses join operation                                        |
| -does not use group by, aggregate functions usually    | -can use group by, having, aggregate functions              |
| -easier to insert, update and delete data through view | -insert, update and delete may be difficult or not possible |
| -simple query structure                                | -complex query structure                                    |
| -used for basic data display                           | -used for reports and combined data                         |
| -faster than complex view                              | -may be slower than simple view                             |
| -example: customer details view                        | -example: invoice report view                               |


6. Index
Definition:
An index is used to speed up data retrieval operations in a database by creating a quick lookup for columns.

Syntax:
create index StudentInfo on Student(name);

Example:
create index idx_name on table_name(column_name);

#Difference between clustered index and non-clustered index

| Clustered Index                              | Non-Clustered Index                            |
| -------------------------------------------  | ---------------------------------------------  |
| -Stores data rows physically in sorted order | -Stores index separately with pointers to data |
| -Only one clustered index                    | -Multiple non-clustered indexes                |
| -Faster for range searches                   | -Faster for specific lookups                   |
| -Changes physical order of table data        | -Does not change table data order              |
| -Usually on Primary Key                      | -Created manually on other columns             |



 